Yes. Below is a VBA example that connects to SQL Server using SQL Server Authentication, executes the stored procedure tblauth_spo_data, and writes the returned data into an Excel worksheet.

1. What you need to change

Replace these values:

YOUR_SERVER_NAME → SQL Server name/IP

YOUR_DATABASE_NAME → Database name

YOUR_USERNAME → SQL Server login

YOUR_PASSWORD → SQL Server password

Sheet1 → Your destination worksheet


2. VBA Macro

Option Explicit

Sub GetSPODataFromSQLServer()

    Dim conn As Object
    Dim cmd As Object
    Dim rs As Object
    
    Dim ws As Worksheet
    Dim serverName As String
    Dim databaseName As String
    Dim username As String
    Dim password As String
    
    Dim connectionString As String
    
    On Error GoTo ErrorHandler
    
    '=========================================
    ' SQL SERVER SETTINGS
    '=========================================
    serverName = "YOUR_SERVER_NAME"
    databaseName = "YOUR_DATABASE_NAME"
    username = "YOUR_USERNAME"
    password = "YOUR_PASSWORD"
    
    '=========================================
    ' DESTINATION WORKSHEET
    '=========================================
    Set ws = ThisWorkbook.Worksheets("Sheet1")
    
    '=========================================
    ' SQL SERVER CONNECTION STRING
    '=========================================
    connectionString = _
        "Provider=SQLOLEDB;" & _
        "Data Source=" & serverName & ";" & _
        "Initial Catalog=" & databaseName & ";" & _
        "User ID=" & username & ";" & _
        "Password=" & password & ";"
    
    '=========================================
    ' CREATE CONNECTION
    '=========================================
    Set conn = CreateObject("ADODB.Connection")
    
    conn.CommandTimeout = 60
    conn.Open connectionString
    
    '=========================================
    ' CREATE COMMAND
    '=========================================
    Set cmd = CreateObject("ADODB.Command")
    
    With cmd
        .ActiveConnection = conn
        .CommandType = 4 ' adCmdStoredProc
        .CommandText = "tblauth_spo_data"
        .CommandTimeout = 60
    End With
    
    '=========================================
    ' EXECUTE STORED PROCEDURE
    '=========================================
    Set rs = cmd.Execute
    
    '=========================================
    ' CLEAR OLD DATA
    '=========================================
    ws.Cells.ClearContents
    
    '=========================================
    ' WRITE COLUMN HEADERS
    '=========================================
    Dim i As Long
    
    For i = 0 To rs.Fields.Count - 1
        ws.Cells(1, i + 1).Value = rs.Fields(i).Name
    Next i
    
    '=========================================
    ' WRITE DATA TO EXCEL
    '=========================================
    If Not rs.EOF Then
        ws.Range("A2").CopyFromRecordset rs
    End If
    
    '=========================================
    ' FORMAT
    '=========================================
    ws.Rows(1).Font.Bold = True
    ws.Columns.AutoFit
    
    MsgBox "Data successfully retrieved from SQL Server.", _
           vbInformation, "SQL Server Data"
    
    '=========================================
    ' CLEAN UP
    '=========================================
    rs.Close
    conn.Close
    
    Set rs = Nothing
    Set cmd = Nothing
    Set conn = Nothing
    
    Exit Sub

ErrorHandler:

    MsgBox "Error retrieving data from SQL Server." & vbCrLf & vbCrLf & _
           "Error Number: " & Err.Number & vbCrLf & _
           "Description: " & Err.Description, _
           vbCritical, "SQL Server Error"
    
    On Error Resume Next
    
    If Not rs Is Nothing Then
        If rs.State = 1 Then rs.Close
    End If
    
    If Not conn Is Nothing Then
        If conn.State = 1 Then conn.Close
    End If
    
    Set rs = Nothing
    Set cmd = Nothing
    Set conn = Nothing

End Sub

Example configuration

For example, if your SQL Server is:

Server: 192.168.1.100
Database: EPPI
Username: sa
Password: MyPassword123

you would set:

serverName = "192.168.1.100"
databaseName = "EPPI"
username = "sa"
password = "MyPassword123"

The macro will essentially perform:

EXEC tblauth_spo_data

and put the result into Excel like:

ID	Name	Section	Email

1	Juan Dela Cruz	IT	juan@email.com
2	Maria Santos	HR	maria@email.com


If your stored procedure has parameters

If tblauth_spo_data requires parameters, for example:

EXEC tblauth_spo_data @Section = 'IT'

then the VBA needs to add the parameter:

cmd.Parameters.Append cmd.CreateParameter( _
    "@Section", 200, 1, 100, "IT")

where 200 is adVarChar and 1 is adParamInput.

